dialogue_history.argument:
AG2 adds a new harm category: student psychological distress/trauma, plus the idea that “opt-out” still leaves students exposed to upsetting classroom norms.

I disagree that this justifies banning dissection outright.

**New rebuttal (trauma-informed implementation):** Student wellbeing concerns can be addressed through concrete classroom design choices—not by eliminating the option. Schools can:
- offer **pre-announced opt-out/opt-in** and require **explicit consent**;
- place alternative work in a **separate station/classroom area** so opting out doesn’t mean watching peers dissect;
- provide **equivalent non-animal labs** (3D/virtual/synthetic tissue) that cover the same objectives;
- use **teacher-led or limited student handling** where appropriate to reduce exposure and distress;
- apply a **trauma-informed approach** similar to other sensitive lab topics.

If a method can be implemented in a way that protects students who are distressed, then psychological discomfort alone shouldn’t become a reason to prohibit dissection for everyone.

**Updated stance:** Students should be allowed to dissect animals as an educational option **only with trauma-informed safeguards**—clear consent, non-coercive opt-out alternatives, and reduced exposure for students who choose not to participate—while keeping dissection available for students who want the hands-on learning.
